This article contains the DotKernel cache layer configuration guide.

The DotKernel Caching Layer is based on Zend Framework Cache, more configuration options can be found at the following links:
<ul>
    <li><a title="Zend Framework Cache Frontends" href="http://framework.zend.com/manual/1.12/en/zend.cache.frontends.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Zend Framework Cache Frontends</a></li>
    <li><a title="Zend Framework Cache Backends" href="http://framework.zend.com/manual/1.12/en/zend.cache.backends.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Zend Framework Cache Backends</a></li>
</ul>
<!--more-->
<h2>Main cache settings (Cache Frontend)</h2>
The main cache settings within the <strong>application.ini </strong>file should look like this:
<pre>>cache.enable = true
cache.factory = "apc"
cache.lifetime = "86400"
cache.namespace = "dotkernel"</pre>
The <em>cache.enable</em> option can be used to disable caching, mostly used in development stage.

The <em>cache.factory</em> value will be the cache service we want to use: <em><strong>file</strong></em> or <em><strong>apc</strong></em>

The <em>cache.namespace </em>will be the cache variables prefix and the <em>cache.lifetime</em> value will define how long the variables cached will be usable before they will need to be re-cached.

&nbsp;
<h2>Individual cache settings (Cache Backend)</h2>
The individual cache settings are optional but we highly recommend that you have theese values set, otherwise other projects might use the same cache
<pre>>; file caching settings
 cache.file.cache_dir = APPLICATION_PATH "/cache"
 cache.file.cache_file_perm = 0600</pre>
&nbsp;

For more settings and caching alternatives see the Zend Framework Cache Links at the article beginning.

The setting pattern and sample are below:
<pre>>cache.BACKEND_NAME.SETTING = "VALUE"
; example:
cache.file.file_name_prefix = "DotKernel"</pre>

